# Instructions

Assess the entire target source at checks.{{ name }}. Is this block an author/editor/translator attribution or signature attached to a nearby main reading section, rather than page furniture or an explanatory footnote?

Use complete local_raw_evidence for the target, the neighboring section headings and text, and placement. A standalone signature after the end of an entry, article, foreword or its bibliography can be main reading content. Personal initials can serve as such a signature; do not require their expanded name.

The whole block must function as the attribution. Merely mentioning an author inside a long footnote, listing citations, or ending a mixed body/photograph-caption block with a credit is not sufficient. Do not certify a mixed block as an attribution.

Page numbers, chapter folios, repeated running headers and isolated variables are not authorial signatures. Capital letters or periods alone do not prove authorship; establish the block function from its local section placement. An ancillary photo credit is not an author signature for a main reading section.

No prior model score is provided. Do not evaluate paragraph joins, word spelling, or whether the section should be reformatted. If a main-reading attribution cannot be established, answer FALSE.

# True

The complete source functions as an author/editor/translator attribution or signature for a nearby main reading section.

# False

That whole-source main-reading attribution function is not established; it may be page furniture, a footnote, mixed text or another kind of content.
